{
  "id": "toronto_jamaican_soul_reggae",
  "name": "Toronto Jamaican-Canadian soul and reggae",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Music of the Jamaican community that grew in Toronto after Canada opened its immigration to the Caribbean in the 1960s, when Kingston musicians including keyboardist Jackie Mittoo and singer Leroy Sibbles settled in the city and worked its clubs and sessions. Local bands mixed rocksteady and reggae rhythms with American soul and funk, a sound gathered on the Light in the Attic reissue Jamaica to Toronto (2006), and from the 1980s bands such as Messenjah made Canadian reggae a recording scene of its own.",
  "recipe": "classic North American mid-1960s toronto jamaican-canadian soul and reggae, bass System, reggae diaspora branch. solo with instrumental accompaniment. pocket fingerstyle locked electric bass, drum kit, single coil electric guitar, pocket ostinato tonewheel organ, tenor ensemble saxophone, bach stradivarius 180 canonical American orchestral ensemble trumpet. live tracking. large-diaphragm condenser, API 312 op-amp pre, API op-amp console, 1176 FET, API proportional-Q EQ. Studer 30ips tape plate reverb.",
